    {
      "id": "C02ca0TVukA1eaBuEhEx-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 230,
      "y": 108,
      "width": 1895.0000000000002,
      "height": 1225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 909237063,
      "version": 693,
      "versionNonce": 611919271,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794132266850,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Functional Requirements\n    1. Limit how many times a user sees an ad within a time window.\n    2. Support multiple caps, e.g. 3 impressions/day and 10/week.\n    3. Enforce caps across devices and profiles linked to the same identity.\n    4. Track impression events and update counts.\n    5. Support advertiser-defined cap policies and time-window semantics.\n    6. Provide reporting and reconciliation for delivered impressions.\n\n2. Non-Functional Requirements\n    1. Ultra-Low Latency: The \"Should I serve this ad?\" check must complete in < 5ms on the hot path. A strongly consistent global read is mathematically impossible within this latency budget\nacross regions.\n    2. High Availability: The ad-serving engine must not block video playback if the capping system degrades.\n    3. Strict Error Boundaries (Over-serving vs. Under-serving): Over-serving violates advertiser contracts and results in unpaid \"make-goods.\" Under-serving leaves inventory unmonetized. The\nsystem must lean toward under-serving (strict contract adherence) during ambiguity.\n\n3. Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation\n    1. Scale: 100 Million ad-supported subscribers.\n    2. Throughput: 10 ad breaks per day = 1 Billion ad requests/day = ~12,000 QPS average, ~40,000 QPS peak.\n    3. Memory (Hot Path): Tracking 1,000 active campaigns per user using standard integers (4 bytes) = 4KB per user. 100M users * 4KB = ~400 GB total global state (easily fits in a sharded\nRedis cluster).\n\n4. API Designing :\n    1. POST /ads/filter : {\n                                  \"account_id\": \"A123\",\n                                  \"profile_id\": \"P1\",\n                                  \"candidate_ad_ids\": [\"Ad1\", \"Ad2\", \"Ad3\"]\n                                }\n    2. POST /ads/track : {\n                                  \"event_id\": \"E123\",\n                                  \"account_id\": \"A123\",\n                                  \"profile_id\": \"P1\",\n                                  \"ad_id\": \"Ad1\",\n                                  \"timestamp\": 1718293049,\n                                  \"region\": \"us-east-1\"\n                                }\n\n    3. PUT /campaigns/{campaign_id}/frequency-cap : {\n                                                                      \"max_per_day\": 3,\n                                                                      \"max_per_week\": 10,\n                                                                      \"window_type\": \"calendar\"\n                                                                    }\n                                                                    \n5. Data Models and entities : \n    1. Filter - account_id, profile_id, candidate_ad_ids\n    2. Track - event_id, account_id, profile_id, ad_id, timestamp, region\n    3. frequency - max+_per_day, max_per_week, window_type\n\n\n6. High level design : ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Functional Requirements\n    1. Limit how many times a user sees an ad within a time window.\n    2. Support multiple caps, e.g. 3 impressions/day and 10/week.\n    3. Enforce caps across devices and profiles linked to the same identity.\n    4. Track impression events and update counts.\n    5. Support advertiser-defined cap policies and time-window semantics.\n    6. Provide reporting and reconciliation for delivered impressions.\n\n2. Non-Functional Requirements\n    1. Ultra-Low Latency: The \"Should I serve this ad?\" check must complete in < 5ms on the hot path. A strongly consistent global read is mathematically impossible within this latency budget across regions.\n    2. High Availability: The ad-serving engine must not block video playback if the capping system degrades.\n    3. Strict Error Boundaries (Over-serving vs. Under-serving): Over-serving violates advertiser contracts and results in unpaid \"make-goods.\" Under-serving leaves inventory unmonetized. The system must lean toward under-serving (strict contract adherence) during ambiguity.\n\n3. Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation\n    1. Scale: 100 Million ad-supported subscribers.\n    2. Throughput: 10 ad breaks per day = 1 Billion ad requests/day = ~12,000 QPS average, ~40,000 QPS peak.\n    3. Memory (Hot Path): Tracking 1,000 active campaigns per user using standard integers (4 bytes) = 4KB per user. 100M users * 4KB = ~400 GB total global state (easily fits in a sharded Redis cluster).\n\n4. API Designing :\n    1. POST /ads/filter : {\n                                  \"account_id\": \"A123\",\n                                  \"profile_id\": \"P1\",\n                                  \"candidate_ad_ids\": [\"Ad1\", \"Ad2\", \"Ad3\"]\n                                }\n    2. POST /ads/track : {\n                                  \"event_id\": \"E123\",\n                                  \"account_id\": \"A123\",\n                                  \"profile_id\": \"P1\",\n                                  \"ad_id\": \"Ad1\",\n                                  \"timestamp\": 1718293049,\n                                  \"region\": \"us-east-1\"\n                                }\n\n    3. PUT /campaigns/{campaign_id}/frequency-cap : {\n                                                                      \"max_per_day\": 3,\n                                                                      \"max_per_week\": 10,\n                                                                      \"window_type\": \"calendar\"\n                                                                    }\n                                                                    \n5. Data Models and entities : \n    1. Filter - account_id, profile_id, candidate_ad_ids\n    2. Track - event_id, account_id, profile_id, ad_id, timestamp, region\n    3. frequency - max+_per_day, max_per_week, window_type\n\n\n6. High level design : ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
