    {
      "id": "u_qkVLAb8Xbt6Eo-mXhNZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 325,
      "y": 2093,
      "width": 1819,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ak",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 540043431,
      "version": 165,
      "versionNonce": 2075797095,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794132950996,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "7. Deep dive and bottlenecks : \n    1. Identity Resolution Across Profiles & Shared Accounts\n    When a request arrives from an anonymous Smart TV, the Ad Decision Engine first calls an internal Identity Graph. If the cap contract is \"10 times per household,\" the counter is\nkeyed by account_id. If the contract is \"3 times per viewer,\" it is keyed by profile_id. To prevent cross-profile leakage, the Local Capping Service maintains nested prefix keys (e.g.,\nhashing both the account and profile).\n    2. Regional Partitions and The \"Split-Brain\" Dilemma\n    If us-east-1 and eu-west-1 lose network connectivity, global Kafka mirroring stops.\n    3. During the partition: A user traveling on a plane (or using a VPN) might watch Netflix in US-East and then EU-West. Because the regions cannot synchronize, the user might see\nthe ad 3 times in the US and 3 times in the EU (total 6 times, violating the cap of 3).\n    4. The Fallback: We explicitly accept this regional over-serving as a rare edge case to maintain high availability (video playback must not break).\n    5. Reconciliation & Billing: When the partition heals, Kafka MirrorMaker pushes the delayed impressions to the Global Billing Engine. The Billing Engine uses exact event-sourcing (not\na Count-Min Sketch). It deduplicates the impression IDs, sorts them by timestamp, and counts them. It bills the advertiser for the first 3 impressions. The remaining 3 are swallowed by\nNetflix as unpaid \"make-goods.\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "7. Deep dive and bottlenecks : \n    1. Identity Resolution Across Profiles & Shared Accounts\n    When a request arrives from an anonymous Smart TV, the Ad Decision Engine first calls an internal Identity Graph. If the cap contract is \"10 times per household,\" the counter is keyed by account_id. If the contract is \"3 times per viewer,\" it is keyed by profile_id. To prevent cross-profile leakage, the Local Capping Service maintains nested prefix keys (e.g., hashing both the account and profile).\n    2. Regional Partitions and The \"Split-Brain\" Dilemma\n    If us-east-1 and eu-west-1 lose network connectivity, global Kafka mirroring stops.\n    3. During the partition: A user traveling on a plane (or using a VPN) might watch Netflix in US-East and then EU-West. Because the regions cannot synchronize, the user might see the ad 3 times in the US and 3 times in the EU (total 6 times, violating the cap of 3).\n    4. The Fallback: We explicitly accept this regional over-serving as a rare edge case to maintain high availability (video playback must not break).\n    5. Reconciliation & Billing: When the partition heals, Kafka MirrorMaker pushes the delayed impressions to the Global Billing Engine. The Billing Engine uses exact event-sourcing (not a Count-Min Sketch). It deduplicates the impression IDs, sorts them by timestamp, and counts them. It bills the advertiser for the first 3 impressions. The remaining 3 are swallowed by Netflix as unpaid \"make-goods.\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
